<h2>Security Maintenance: The Foundation That Keeps Your Business Online</h2>

<p>Security maintenance is not optional for contractors. It is the difference between a website that generates leads 24/7 and a website that displays malware warnings to every visitor. Home service contractors face specific security risks because their sites often collect personal information (names, addresses, phone numbers, photos of home interiors) and process payments. A breach does not just hurt your website. It exposes customer data, violates trust, and can trigger legal liability under state data privacy laws.</p>

<h3>The Real Security Threats Contractor Websites Face</h3>

<p>Let us be specific about what threatens contractor websites. Generic advice about "staying safe online" does not help. Here are the actual attack vectors we monitor and defend against:</p>

<p><strong>Brute Force Login Attacks.</strong> Automated bots attempt thousands of username and password combinations against your WordPress login page. A standard WordPress site without protection faces roughly 90 brute force attempts per day, according to Wordfence's 2024 threat report. Most contractors use weak passwords like "Plumber123" or leave the default "admin" username. Bots crack these in minutes.</p>

<p><strong>Plugin and Theme Vulnerabilities.</strong> Popular plugins are high-value targets because one exploit affects thousands of sites. In 2024, vulnerabilities were disclosed in widely used plugins including Contact Form 7, WooCommerce, and Elementor. Contractors running auto-updates without testing sometimes break their sites. Contractors not updating at all leave known vulnerabilities open for weeks or months.</p>

<p><strong>SQL Injection and Cross-Site Scripting (XSS).</strong> These technical attacks exploit poorly coded forms and input fields. A vulnerable contact form can allow an attacker to inject malicious code that steals visitor data or redirects traffic to scam sites. We have seen contractor sites where the contact form was silently forwarding lead information to a third party for months before discovery.</p>

<p><strong>Malware Injections.</strong> Once a site is compromised, attackers inject malicious code that can redirect visitors to gambling sites, display unwanted ads, or install cryptocurrency miners on visitors' computers. Google blacklists approximately 10,000 websites per day for malware. Getting removed from Google's blacklist takes 1 to 3 days even after cleanup, meaning days of lost traffic and leads.</p>

<p><strong>Phishing Pages.</strong> Attackers sometimes create hidden pages on compromised contractor sites designed to steal login credentials from other services (banking, email, social media). Your domain reputation gets destroyed, and your site may be flagged by every major browser and security tool.</p>

