<h3>SSL Certificates: The Security Signal Customers Notice</h3>

<p>SSL certificates encrypt data between your website and visitors. They also display the padlock icon in browsers. Since 2018, Google Chrome marks all HTTP sites as "Not Secure." For contractors, this is devastating. A homeowner researching "HVAC repair near me" sees "Not Secure" and immediately bounces to a competitor.</p>

<p>SSL certificates expire. Let us say that again because it surprises contractors: SSL certificates expire. Most last 1 year. When they expire, the padlock disappears, browsers show warnings, and some hosting providers suspend sites entirely. Maintenance includes monitoring expiration dates and renewing certificates before they lapse. We have seen contractors lose a full week of leads because their SSL certificate expired over a holiday weekend and nobody was monitoring it.</p>

<h3>Web Application Firewalls (WAF): Your First Line of Defense</h3>

<p>A Web Application Firewall filters traffic before it reaches your website. It blocks known malicious IP addresses, prevents SQL injection attempts, stops brute force attacks, and filters out bot traffic. For contractor websites, WAFs are particularly valuable because they reduce server load. A plumbing site getting 200 real visitors per day might face 5,000 bot requests. Without a WAF, those bot requests consume server resources, slow the site for real visitors, and increase hosting costs.</p>

<p>Cloudflare's free plan includes basic WAF protection. Professional maintenance plans use premium WAF rules tailored to WordPress and contractor-specific threats. The difference is significant: basic WAF blocks generic attacks; tuned WAF blocks the specific exploit patterns targeting your plugins and themes.</p>

