<h2>Backup and Recovery: The Insurance Policy Every Contractor Needs</h2>

<p>Backups are your safety net. When updates break your site, when malware infects your files, when a developer makes a critical error, backups let you restore functionality quickly. Without them, you are one mistake away from losing everything.</p>

<p>We have seen contractors lose entire websites because their hosting company's "backup" was a single weekly snapshot that overwrote the previous one. When their site broke on Friday, the Saturday backup captured the broken state. There was no clean version to restore. They paid $3,000 for emergency reconstruction.</p>

<h3>The 3-2-1 Backup Rule for Contractor Websites</h3>

<p>Professional backup strategy follows the 3-2-1 rule: 3 copies of your data, on 2 different media types, with 1 copy stored offsite. For contractor websites, this translates to:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Primary copy:</strong> Your live website on the hosting server</li>
<li><strong>Secondary copy:</strong> Automated backups stored on the same hosting account (for quick restores)</li>
<li><strong>Tertiary copy:</strong> Offsite backups stored on a separate service (Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage, or Dropbox) in a different geographic location</li>
</ul>

<p>This redundancy protects against server failure, hosting company errors, and even regional disasters. If your hosting data center floods, your offsite backup remains safe.</p>

<h3>What a Complete Website Backup Includes</h3>

<p>Many contractors think their hosting provider "backs everything up." In reality, hosting backups often miss critical components. A complete contractor website backup includes:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Database:</strong> All content, user accounts, form submissions, orders, and settings</li>
<li><strong>WordPress files:</strong> Core files, themes, plugins, and uploads</li>
<li><strong>Media library:</strong> All images, videos, PDFs, and documents</li>
<li><strong>Custom code:</strong> Theme modifications, custom CSS, and custom functions</li>
<li><strong>Configuration files:</strong> .htaccess, wp-config.php, and server settings</li>
<li><strong>DNS records:</strong> Domain configuration and email routing settings</li>
<li><strong>SSL certificates:</strong> Certificate files and private keys</li>
</ul>

<p>Partial backups that only capture the database leave you with content but no design. File-only backups preserve appearance but lose all content. Complete backups capture everything needed for full restoration.</p>

<h3>Backup Frequency: Matching Schedule to Risk</h3>

<p>Backup frequency should match how often your site changes. Here is a practical framework:</p>

