<h3>Testing Backups: The Step Most People Skip</h3>

<p>A backup you cannot restore is worthless. Professional maintenance includes periodic backup testing: restoring a backup to a staging environment and verifying that the site functions correctly. This catches corrupted backups, incomplete captures, and compatibility issues before an emergency occurs.</p>

<p>We test backups quarterly for all maintenance clients. In 2024, we discovered that 12% of automated backups from a popular hosting provider were incomplete due to a server configuration issue. Because we tested, we caught the problem and switched backup methods before any client needed an emergency restore.</p>

<h3>Disaster Recovery Planning</h3>

<p>Beyond backups, disaster recovery means having a documented plan for common scenarios:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Site completely down:</strong> Who is contacted? What is the first troubleshooting step? How long until restore begins?</li>
<li><strong>Malware infection:</strong> Is the site taken offline immediately? Is a clean backup restored, or is malware cleaned from current files?</li>
<li><strong>Hosting company failure:</strong> Can the site be migrated to new hosting within hours using existing backups?</li>
<li><strong>Domain expiration or DNS hijacking:</strong> Who manages domain renewal? What is the recovery process if DNS is compromised?</li>
</ul>

<p>Professional website maintenance services include documented disaster recovery procedures. You should know exactly what happens and who does what when problems arise.</p>

