<h3>Which Tier Is Right for Your Contracting Business?</h3>

<p>The right plan depends on your website's complexity, your lead volume, and your risk tolerance. Here is our practical guidance:</p>

<p><strong>DIY/Basic ($29-$79/month):</strong> Suitable for solo contractors with simple brochure sites, low traffic (under 100 visits per month), and high technical comfort. You handle updates yourself using tools like ManageWP or InfiniteWP. Risk: if you forget to update for 3 months, vulnerabilities accumulate. If a plugin breaks your site, you troubleshoot alone.</p>

<p><strong>Standard ($100-$249/month):</strong> The sweet spot for most established contractors. You get professional management of updates, security, backups, and basic support. Your site stays secure and functional without your direct involvement. Includes limited content updates for seasonal changes or team updates. This tier protects a website investment of $3,000 to $10,000 effectively.</p>

<p><strong>Professional ($250-$499/month):</strong> For contractors with complex sites (multiple service areas, appointment booking, payment processing), high traffic (1,000+ visits per month), or significant revenue dependence on the website. Includes faster support, more content update hours, and proactive optimization. Appropriate for contractors generating $10,000+ monthly from web leads.</p>

<p><strong>Enterprise ($500+/month):</strong> For multi-location contractors, franchises, or contractors with custom web applications. Includes dedicated resources, custom development support, and strategic consultation. Typically paired with ongoing SEO and digital marketing services.</p>

<h3>Red Flags in Maintenance Plan Pricing</h3>

<p>Be wary of these pricing traps:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>"Unlimited" support at very low prices:</strong> Unlimited does not exist. Low-priced unlimited plans either have hidden limits, slow response times, or cut corners on quality.</li>
<li><strong>Plans that exclude plugin updates:</strong> Plugin updates are where most vulnerabilities live. A plan that only updates WordPress core is incomplete.</li>
<li><strong>No mention of backup testing:</strong> Backups without testing are hopes, not guarantees.</li>
<li><strong>Long-term contracts with no exit clause:</strong> Quality maintenance providers do not need to lock you in. Month-to-month or short-term contracts show confidence.</li>
<li><strong>Hidden fees for "emergency" support:</strong> Website problems are emergencies by nature. Charging extra for urgent issues suggests the provider is not actually managing your site proactively.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The True Cost of Cheap Maintenance</h3>

<p>A $49 per month plan that misses a critical plugin vulnerability costs you far more than a $199 plan that prevents the breach. When evaluating cost, consider:</p>

<ul>
<li>Value of one day's leads lost to downtime</li>
<li>Cost of emergency cleanup versus preventive maintenance</li>
<li>Your time spent managing updates versus focusing on your trade</li>
<li>Reputation damage from a hacked or broken site</li>
</ul>

<p>Most contractors find that professional maintenance pays for itself by preventing a single significant incident per year.</p>

